  [1mMy recommendation: native ES modules.[0m It's the option that makes "other forms will need it later" a one-line import
  instead of a load-order problem, and it costs you one command to serve the page instead of double-clicking the file.
  The global approach is the one that gets more expensive with exactly the growth you described.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the session store be shared with app.js and future forms? → Native ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Recorded: native ES modules, with a [38;5;153msrc/session.js[39m.

  Last question before I lay out approaches. "Track who logged in" could mean just the ID, or enough to actually show
  and audit it — and since later forms will read whatever shape this is, the stored record is the interface they'll
  depend on.

  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m only[0m — the store holds one string. Smallest possible surface; anything else a form needs (a display name,
    when the login happened) has to be fetched or re-derived. Adding fields later is easy; [3mremoving[0m them isn't, so
    starting small is the reversible direction.
  - [1m[38;5;153m{ userId, username }[0m — lets a later form render "Signed in as jane" without a round trip. Costs a duplicated
    username that could drift from the server's record, though with one write path that risk is near zero.
  - [1m[38;5;153m{ userId, username, loginAt }[0m — adds a timestamp, which is what makes "track" auditable and gives you a hook for
    session expiry later. Costs a field nothing reads today.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153m{ userId, username, loginAt }[39m.[0m You used the word "track," and a login you can't timestamp isn't
  tracked — it's just a current value. The timestamp is also the precondition for expiring a [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m session,
  which is the most likely next ask after "persist."
